  "worked_solution": "If $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta ,\\,z=z\n$$\n\nCalculating $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\pi ) \\\\  & =5\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $y$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\pi ) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $Q$ are $(-5,0,7).$",
